window.SEARCH=[{"t": "Introduction", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#introduction", "x": ""}, {"t": "1) Define: Data, Database, DBMS, Data Redundancy.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#1-define-data-database-dbms-data-redundancy", "x": "Data: Raw, unprocessed facts and figures (numbers, text, images, etc.) that have no meaning on their own. Example: 101, \"Ravi\", 85. Database: An organised collection of related data, stored electronically so that it can be easily accessed, managed and updated. Example: a university database storing students, courses and marks. DBMS (Database Management System): A software system that allows users to define, create, store, retrieve, update and control a database. It acts as an interface between the database and its users or application programs. Examples: MySQL, Oracle, SQL Server, PostgreSQL. Data Redundancy: The unnecessary duplication of the same data at multiple places. It wastes storage,"}, {"t": "2) Explain the purpose of the database system.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#2-explain-the-purpose-of-the-database-system", "x": "Earlier, organisations stored data in separate file-processing systems (flat files). These systems had many problems. The database system was developed to overcome them. Its main purposes are: Reduce data redundancy and inconsistency by storing each fact once. Avoid data isolation: data kept in one central place instead of scattered files with different formats. Easy data access: queries can be answered quickly using a query language such as SQL. Integrity: constraints (e.g. marks between 0 and 100) are enforced centrally. Atomicity of updates: a transaction is either completed fully or not at all (e.g. fund transfer). Concurrent access: many users can use the data at the same time without c"}, {"t": "3) Explain the purpose and application of DBMS.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#3-explain-the-purpose-and-application-of-dbms", "x": "To store large volumes of data in an organised way. To provide efficient and convenient retrieval and modification of data. To control redundancy, maintain integrity and ensure consistency. To provide security, concurrency control, and backup/recovery. To provide data independence between programs and data."}, {"t": "4) List the benefits of database approach.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#4-list-the-benefits-of-database-approach", "x": "Controlled redundancy: duplication is minimised. Data consistency: a single update reflects everywhere. Data sharing: multiple users and applications share the same data. Data integrity: rules and constraints keep data accurate. Security: access rights restrict unauthorised use. Data independence: changing storage structure does not affect programs. Concurrent access: multi-user environment supported. Backup and recovery: data restored after system failure. Standards enforcement: uniform naming and formats across the organisation. Reduced application development time and improved decision making."}, {"t": "5) Describe various disadvantages of file system compared to Database Management System.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#5-describe-various-disadvantages-of-file-system-compared-to-database-management-", "x": "Data redundancy and inconsistency: the same data is stored in many files; changes in one file may not be made in others. Difficulty in accessing data: every new query requires a new application program to be written. Data isolation: data is scattered in files of different formats, making it hard to retrieve. Integrity problems: constraints are buried in program code; adding new constraints means changing programs. Atomicity problems: if a system fails midway through an operation (e.g. transfer of money), the data may be left in an inconsistent state. Concurrent access anomalies: two users updating the same data simultaneously can produce wrong results. Security problems: it is hard to give d"}, {"t": "6) Discuss disadvantages of file processing systems and advantages of DBMS (differentiate it).", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#6-discuss-disadvantages-of-file-processing-systems-and-advantages-of-dbms-differ", "x": "A DBMS removes the major drawbacks of file systems (redundancy, inconsistency, poor security and concurrency) and is preferred for medium and large applications. File systems may still be used for very small, simple applications."}, {"t": "7) Explain different database users.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#7-explain-different-database-users", "x": "Database users are classified according to how they interact with the system: Naive (parametric) users: unsophisticated users who interact through ready-made application interfaces, without knowing about the DBMS. Examples: bank tellers, ATM users, railway reservation clerks, online shoppers. Application programmers: computer professionals who write application programs using languages such as Java, Python or C++ with embedded SQL, to build the interfaces used by naive users. Sophisticated users: engineers, scientists and analysts who write their own queries directly in a query language (SQL) without writing programs. Example: a data analyst running OLAP queries. Specialised users: write spe"}, {"t": "8) What are the responsibilities of a DBA?", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#8-what-are-the-responsibilities-of-a-dba", "x": "The Database Administrator (DBA) is the person who has central control over both the data and the programs that access it. Responsibilities: Designing the logical and physical structure of the database (schema definition). Deciding the storage structure and access methods. Granting authorisation and controlling user access (security). Taking regular backups and planning recovery from failure. Monitoring performance and tuning the database. Ensuring data integrity and consistency. Modifying the schema and physical organisation when requirements change. Installing, upgrading and maintaining DBMS software. Routine maintenance such as freeing disk space and checking free space."}, {"t": "9) List the major functions performed by DBA.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#9-list-the-major-functions-performed-by-dba", "x": "Schema definition: creating the original database schema using DDL. Storage structure and access-method definition. Schema and physical-organisation modification. Granting of authorisation for data access. Routine maintenance: periodic backup, ensuring enough free disk space, monitoring jobs and performance. Security enforcement and user management. Backup and recovery management."}, {"t": "10) What is schema and instance? Explain with example.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#10-what-is-schema-and-instance-explain-with-example", "x": "Schema: The overall logical design or structure of the database. It describes tables, attributes, data types and relationships. It is specified at design time and changes rarely. It is also called the intension (or skeleton) of the database. Instance: The actual data stored in the database at a particular moment in time. It changes frequently as data is inserted, updated or deleted. It is also called the extension or database state. STUDENT(Roll_no, Name, Course, Semester) <- SCHEMA The table above, with its two rows, is an instance of the STUDENT schema. If a new row is added, the instance changes, but the schema stays the same."}, {"t": "11) Explain three level architecture of database system.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#11-explain-three-level-architecture-of-database-system", "x": "The three-schema architecture (ANSI-SPARC) separates the user's view of data from its physical storage. It has three levels: External level (View level): the highest level. It describes only the part of the database relevant to a particular user group. Many different views can exist. It hides the rest of the data and adds security. Conceptual level (Logical level): describes what data is stored and the relationships among the data for the entire community of users. It hides storage details. The DBA works at this level. Internal level (Physical level): the lowest level. It describes how data is physically stored on disk: file organisation, indexes, record placement, compression. External/Conc"}, {"t": "12) Explain three levels of data abstraction in DBMS.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#12-explain-three-levels-of-data-abstraction-in-dbms", "x": "Data abstraction means hiding complex storage details from users and showing only the necessary information. DBMS provides three levels: The three levels give a simple, secure and flexible way to interact with a database, regardless of its internal complexity."}, {"t": "13) What is data independence? Explain the difference between physical and logical data independence with example.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#13-what-is-data-independence-explain-the-difference-between-physical-and-logical", "x": "Data independence is the ability to modify the schema at one level of the database system without having to change the schema at the next higher level. It is achieved through the three-level architecture. The ability to change the internal (physical) schema without changing the conceptual schema or application programs. Example: changing a file organisation from sequential to indexed, moving data to a new disk, or adding an index does not require any change to the table definitions or application programs. The ability to change the conceptual (logical) schema without changing external schemas or application programs. Example: adding a new column Email to the STUDENT table, or splitting a tab"}, {"t": "14) Explain database system architecture with diagram in detail.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#14-explain-database-system-architecture-with-diagram-in-detail", "x": "A database system is partitioned into modules that deal with each of its responsibilities. The main components are the users, the query processor, the storage manager and the disk storage. DDL interpreter: interprets DDL statements and records definitions in the data dictionary. DML compiler: translates DML statements into an evaluation plan of low-level instructions, and performs query optimisation to choose the cheapest plan. Query evaluation engine: executes the low-level instructions generated by the DML compiler. Embedded DML precompiler: converts DML statements embedded in application programs into normal procedure calls. It is the interface between the low-level data stored on disk an"}, {"t": "15) Explain database system 3 tier architecture with diagram in detail.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#15-explain-database-system-3-tier-architecture-with-diagram-in-detail", "x": "In a three-tier architecture, the client does not directly communicate with the database. Instead, the client machine acts as a front end and an application server sits in between. It is widely used for web-based applications. Presentation tier (client): the user interface (web browser or mobile app). It takes user input and displays results. It has no direct access to the database. Application / Business-logic tier: the middle layer. It contains business rules and processing logic, validates requests, and communicates with both client and database. Examples: Tomcat, Node.js, Django servers. Data tier (database server): the DBMS that stores, retrieves and manages data (MySQL, Oracle). It rec"}, {"t": "Entity-Relationship model", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#entity-relationship-model", "x": ""}, {"t": "1) What is Entity-Relationship model? Explain the steps to reduce the ER diagram to ER database schema.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#1-what-is-entity-relationship-model-explain-the-steps-to-reduce-the-er-diagram-t", "x": "The Entity-Relationship (E-R) model is a high-level conceptual data model proposed by Peter Chen (1976). It represents the real world as a collection of entities (objects), their attributes (properties) and the relationships among them. The result is drawn as an E-R diagram, which is easy to understand for both designers and users, and can be converted into a relational schema. Entity: a real-world object that is distinguishable from others (Student, Book). Attribute: a property that describes an entity (Name, Roll_no). Relationship: an association among two or more entities (Student enrolls in Course)."}, {"t": "2) What is mapping cardinalities? Explain it with real time examples.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#2-what-is-mapping-cardinalities-explain-it-with-real-time-examples", "x": "Mapping cardinality (cardinality ratio) expresses the number of entities of one entity set that can be associated with entities of another entity set through a relationship set. It is most useful for binary relationships."}, {"t": "3) Explain types of attribute with example.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#3-explain-types-of-attribute-with-example", "x": "An attribute is a property or characteristic of an entity. Each attribute has a domain (set of permitted values). Simple (atomic) attribute: cannot be divided further. Example: Roll_no, Age, Salary. Composite attribute: can be divided into smaller sub-parts with independent meaning. Example: Name → First_name, Middle_name, Last_name; Address → Street, City, Pin. Single-valued attribute: has only one value for an entity. Example: Roll_no, DOB. Multivalued attribute: can hold more than one value for one entity. Drawn as a double ellipse. Example: Phone_no, Email_id, Skills. Derived attribute: its value is calculated from other attributes; drawn with a dashed ellipse. Example: Age derived from "}, {"t": "4) Explain Specialization feature of ER diagram with example.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#4-explain-specialization-feature-of-er-diagram-with-example", "x": "Specialization is a top-down design process in which a higher-level entity set is divided into lower-level entity sets that are distinguished by some characteristic. The lower-level entities inherit all attributes and relationships of the higher-level entity and also have their own extra attributes. Represented by a triangle labelled ISA (\"is a\") pointing toward the higher-level entity. Example: PERSON(Person_id, Name, Address) is specialized into STUDENT(Course, Semester) and EMPLOYEE(Salary, Dept). A student is a person. Constraints: disjoint (an entity belongs to at most one lower set) or overlapping (can belong to many); total (every higher-level entity must belong to a lower set) or par"}, {"t": "5) Explain Generalization feature of E-R Diagram.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#5-explain-generalization-feature-of-e-r-diagram", "x": "Generalization is a bottom-up design process. Several lower-level entity sets that share common features are combined into a higher-level entity set. It is the reverse of specialization. The common attributes (Acc_no, Balance) are moved up to the higher-level entity ACCOUNT. Unique attributes (Interest_rate, Overdraft_limit) stay with the lower-level entities. Shown with the same ISA triangle as specialization. Example: CAR and TRUCK both have Reg_no, Owner, Colour; they are generalized as VEHICLE. Purpose: to emphasise similarities, remove duplication of attributes and make the schema simpler."}, {"t": "6) Explain aggregation operation of ER diagram.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#6-explain-aggregation-operation-of-er-diagram", "x": "A limitation of the basic E-R model is that it cannot express a relationship among relationships. Aggregation is an abstraction in which a relationship set (together with the entities it relates) is treated as a single higher-level entity, so that it can take part in another relationship. Represented by drawing a dashed box around the relationship and the entities it connects. Example: An employee works on a project (Works_on). A manager manages each such (employee, project) combination. Since Manages is a relationship between a Manager and the relationship Works_on, we aggregate Works_on into one abstract entity. Why not a ternary relationship? Some (employee, project) pairs may have no man"}, {"t": "7) Draw an ER diagram for exam system of INDUS UNIVERSITY.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#7-draw-an-er-diagram-for-exam-system-of-indus-university", "x": "Entities: Student, Subject, Exam, Faculty, Result. Relationships: Appears_in, Conducted_for, Invigilates, Gets, Of. A Student appears in many exams and an Exam is attended by many students (M:N). Each exam is conducted for one Subject, while a subject can have many exams (N:1). Many Faculty members invigilate an exam and a faculty member invigilates many exams (M:N). A student gets many Results, each result belongs to one student (1:N); each result is of one subject (N:1). STUDENT(Enroll_no, Name, Program) SUBJECT(Sub_code, Sub_name, Credits) EXAM(Exam_id, Exam_type, Date, Sub_code*) FACULTY(Fac_id, Name, Dept) RESULT(Result_id, Marks, Grade, Enroll_no*, Sub_code*) APPE"}, {"t": "8) Construct E-R diagram for a hospital with a set of patients and medical doctors. Associate with each patient a log of various tests and examinations conducted.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#8-construct-e-r-diagram-for-a-hospital-with-a-set-of-patients-and-medical-doctor", "x": "Entities: Doctor, Patient, and Test_Log. Test_Log is a weak entity since a test record has no meaning without the patient it belongs to. It depends on Patient through the identifying relationship Undergoes. Doctor - Treats - Patient: M:N (a doctor treats many patients; a patient may be treated by many doctors). Relationship attribute: Treat_date. Patient - Undergoes - Test_Log: 1:N, with total participation of Test_Log (double line). Key attributes: Doctor_id, Patient_id. Test_Log has partial key Test_no; its full key is Patient_id + Test_no. Phone_no could be multivalued; Age is derived from date of birth."}, {"t": "9) Construct E-R diagram of the bank. It provides different kinds of bank accounts and loans. It operates number of branches.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#9-construct-e-r-diagram-of-the-bank-it-provides-different-kinds-of-bank-accounts", "x": "Entities: Customer, Account (specialized into Savings and Current), Loan, Branch, and the weak entity Payment. Depositor (Customer - Account): M:N, with attribute Access_date. Borrower (Customer - Loan): M:N. Has (Branch - Account): 1:N; Originates (Branch - Loan): 1:N. The bank operates many branches. Loan_payment (Loan - Payment): 1:N identifying relationship; Payment is a weak entity with partial key Pay_no. Account is specialized by ISA into SAVINGS (Interest_rate) and CURRENT (Overdraft_limit) accounts."}, {"t": "10) Draw E-R Diagram for the School Management System.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#10-draw-e-r-diagram-for-the-school-management-system", "x": "Student - Enrolled_in - Class: N:1 (many students are in one class). Class - Includes - Subject: M:N (a class studies many subjects; a subject is taught to many classes). Teacher - Teaches - Subject: N:M (a teacher teaches many subjects and a subject may be taught by many teachers). Student - Pays - Fee: 1:N (a student pays many fee instalments). Keys: Roll_no, Class_id, Sub_code, Teacher_id, Fee_id."}, {"t": "11) Give symbols used in E-R Diagram and Draw the E-R diagram of Library Management System.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#11-give-symbols-used-in-e-r-diagram-and-draw-the-e-r-diagram-of-library-manageme", "x": "Member - Borrows - Book: M:N with attributes Issue_date and Return_date. Book - Published_by - Publisher: N:1. Librarian - Manages - Book: 1:N. Keys: Member_id, ISBN, Staff_id, Pub_name. Phone_no is multivalued."}, {"t": "12) Draw E-R diagram for supplier who supplies different parts. The parts are used in different projects. Explain the mapping cardinality used. Assume suitable attributes.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#12-draw-e-r-diagram-for-supplier-who-supplies-different-parts-the-parts-are-used", "x": "Supplier - Supplies - Part: Many-to-Many (M:N). One supplier can supply many different parts, and one part can be supplied by many suppliers. The relationship attribute is Quantity. Part - Used_in - Project: Many-to-Many (M:N). One part can be used in many projects, and one project uses many parts. The relationship attribute is Qty_used. Both M:N relationships become separate tables: SUPPLIES(S_id, P_id, Quantity) and USED_IN(P_id, Proj_id, Qty_used). Alternative: if the supplier, part and project must be linked together (which supplier supplied which part to which project), a single ternary relationship SUPPLY(S_id, P_id, Proj_id, Quantity) can be used."}, {"t": "13) Construct an E-R Diagram for an insurance company with a set of customers, each of whom owns number of cars, also each can have number of recorded accidents associated with it.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#13-construct-an-e-r-diagram-for-an-insurance-company-with-a-set-of-customers-eac", "x": "Customer - Owns - Car: 1:N. A customer owns many cars; each car belongs to one customer (total participation of Car). Car - Participated - Accident: M:N, with attribute Damage_amount. A car can be involved in many recorded accidents and an accident can involve more than one car. Keys: Cust_id, Reg_no, Report_no. CUSTOMER(Cust_id, Name, Address) CAR(Reg_no, Model, Year, Cust_id*) ACCIDENT(Report_no, Acc_date, Location) PARTICIPATED(Reg_no*, Report_no*, Damage_amount)"}, {"t": "14) What is constraint in database? Explain types of constraints with suitable example.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#14-what-is-constraint-in-database-explain-types-of-constraints-with-suitable-exa", "x": "Constraints are rules enforced on the data in a database to ensure its accuracy, validity and consistency. The DBMS rejects any insert, update or delete that violates a constraint. CREATE TABLE Student ( Roll_no INT PRIMARY KEY, Name VARCHAR(30) NOT NULL, Email VARCHAR(50) UNIQUE, Marks INT CHECK (Marks BETWEEN 0 AND 100), Dept_id INT, FOREIGN KEY (Dept_id) RE Mapping cardinality constraints: 1:1, 1:N, N:1, M:N. Participation constraints: total (every entity must participate, double line) and partial (single line). Generalization constraints: disjoint / overlapping, total / partial."}, {"t": "15) Draw symbols for following in E-R diagram: (a) Weak Entity set, Derived attribute, Multivalued. (b) Relationship Set, and Primary key attribute.", "p": "Unit 1", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-1/#15-draw-symbols-for-following-in-e-r-diagram-a-weak-entity-set-derived-attribute", "x": ""}, {"t": "Relational Model", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#relational-model", "x": ""}, {"t": "1) Explain candidate key, primary key and foreign key.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#1-explain-candidate-key-primary-key-and-foreign-key", "x": "A key is an attribute (or set of attributes) that helps to uniquely identify a tuple (row) in a relation (table). Consider STUDENT(Roll_no, Enroll_no, Email, Name, Dept_id). Candidate key: a minimal set of attributes that can uniquely identify every tuple. A relation can have more than one candidate key. Example: Roll_no, Enroll_no and Email are all candidate keys of STUDENT (each is unique and cannot be reduced further). Primary key: the one candidate key chosen by the designer to identify tuples. It must be unique and NOT NULL, and a table can have only one. Example: Roll_no is chosen as the primary key. The remaining candidate keys (Enroll_no, Email) are called alternate keys. Foreign key"}, {"t": "2) Explain following terms with suitable example: (a) Primary Key (b) Candidate Key (c) Super Key (d) On delete cascade", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#2-explain-following-terms-with-suitable-example-a-primary-key-b-candidate-key-c-", "x": "The attribute (or minimal combination of attributes) chosen to uniquely identify each row of a table. It cannot be NULL and cannot repeat. Example: in EMPLOYEE(Emp_id, Name, Phone), Emp_id is the primary key. A primary key made of two or more attributes is a composite key, e.g. (Roll_no, Course_id) in ENROLLMENT. Every minimal attribute set that can uniquely identify rows is a candidate key. One of them becomes the primary key. Example: In EMPLOYEE(Emp_id, Aadhar_no, Name), both Emp_id and Aadhar_no are candidate keys; Emp_id is selected as the primary key. A set of one or more attributes that uniquely identifies a tuple. It may contain extra (unnecessary) attributes. Every candidate key is "}, {"t": "3) Explain following relational algebra operations: (a) Natural join operation (b) Selection and projection operation", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#3-explain-following-relational-algebra-operations-a-natural-join-operation-b-sel", "x": "Relational algebra is a procedural query language. Operations take one or two relations as input and produce a new relation as output. Consider the sample relations: The natural join of R and S combines tuples that have equal values on all common attributes, and the common attribute appears only once in the result. It is equal to a Cartesian product followed by a selection on the common attributes and then a projection that removes the duplicate column. Notation: R ⋈ S Query: EMPLOYEE ⋈ DEPARTMENT (common attribute: Dept_id) Department D3 (IT) has no matching employee, so it does not appear in the result. Selection selects rows (tuples) that satisfy a condition. Notation: σcondition(R). Exam"}, {"t": "4) List relational algebra operators and explain any two with example.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#4-list-relational-algebra-operators-and-explain-any-two-with-example", "x": "Fundamental operators: select, project, union, set difference, Cartesian product and rename. The others can be derived from them. R ∪ S returns all tuples that are in R, or in S, or in both, with duplicates removed. The relations must be union compatible (same number of attributes with same domains). Example: Let ARTS(Name) = {Ravi, Meena} and SCIENCE(Name) = {Meena, Amit}. Then ARTS ∪ SCIENCE = {Ravi, Meena, Amit}: students who joined at least one club. R − S returns tuples that are in R but not in S (union compatible relations required). Example: ARTS − SCIENCE = {Ravi}: students in ARTS who are not in SCIENCE. Bank example: πCustomer_name(Depositor) ∪ πCustomer_name(Borrower) lists all cu"}, {"t": "5) What is Relational Algebra? Define Relational Algebra operation cross product with example.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#5-what-is-relational-algebra-define-relational-algebra-operation-cross-product-w", "x": "Relational algebra is a procedural query language that consists of a set of operations that take one or two relations as input and produce a new relation as output. It specifies what to retrieve and also how (the order of operations). It forms the theoretical basis of SQL and of query optimisation in a DBMS. The Cartesian product R × S combines every tuple of R with every tuple of S. If R has n tuples and S has m tuples, the result has n × m tuples; if R has a attributes and S has b attributes, the result has a + b attributes. If attribute names clash, they are prefixed with the relation name (R.A, S.A). Notation: R × S = { t q | t ∈ R and q ∈ S } The product by itself gives many meaningless"}, {"t": "6) Explain selection and projection operation with example.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#6-explain-selection-and-projection-operation-with-example", "x": "Selects rows (tuples) that satisfy a given predicate; it is a horizontal subset of the relation. Notation: σpredicate(R). Predicates use =, ≠, <, ≤, >, ≥ and can be combined with ∧ (and), ∨ (or), ¬ (not). Example: σCity = \"Ahmedabad\" ∧ Salary > 35000(EMPLOYEE) returns only the tuple (103, Amit, Ahmedabad, 40000). Selection does not remove columns, and it does not produce duplicates. Selects columns (attributes) from a relation; it is a vertical subset. Duplicate rows are eliminated. Notation: πA1, A2, ...(R). Example: πSalary(EMPLOYEE) returns {30000, 25000, 40000}; 25000 appears once only because duplicates are removed. Example: πName, City(EMPLOYEE) returns (Ravi, Ahmedabad), (Meena, Surat"}, {"t": "7) What is database schema? Explain the select, project, natural join, union and Cartesian product operations.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#7-what-is-database-schema-explain-the-select-project-natural-join-union-and-cart", "x": "Database schema is the logical design (skeleton) of a database. It describes the relations (tables), their attributes with data types, keys and constraints. It is defined once at design time and changes rarely. Example: Branch(branch_name, branch_city) Account(branch_name, acc_no, balance) Depositor(customer_name, acc_no) The actual data in the tables at a moment is the instance. Operations of relational algebra are explained below, using EMPLOYEE(Emp_id, Name, Dept_id, Salary) and DEPARTMENT(Dept_id, Dept_name) from Q3."}, {"t": "8) Consider following schema and represent given statements in relational algebra form.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#8-consider-following-schema-and-represent-given-statements-in-relational-algebra", "x": "Branch(branch_name, branch_city) Account(branch_name, acc_no, balance) Depositor(customer_name, acc_no) πcustomer_name( σbranch_name = \"abc\"( Account ⋈ Depositor ) ) Explanation: Account and Depositor are joined on the common attribute acc_no; rows of branch 'abc' are selected and the customer names are projected. πcustomer_name( σbranch_city = \"Ahmedabad\" ∧ balance > 10000( Branch ⋈ Account ⋈ Depositor ) ) Explanation: Branch ⋈ Account joins on branch_name, and the result is joined with Depositor on acc_no. Both conditions are applied together with ∧. branch_nameGmax(balance)( Account ) Explanation: G is the aggregate (grouping) operator. Account tuples are grouped by branch_name and the ma"}, {"t": "9) Explain transformation of relational expression into equivalent relational expression.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#9-explain-transformation-of-relational-expression-into-equivalent-relational-exp", "x": "Two relational-algebra expressions are equivalent if they produce the same set of tuples on every legal database instance. The query optimiser uses equivalence rules to transform a given expression into another, cheaper one (query optimisation). The order of tuples is not important. Cascade of selections: σθ1 ∧ θ2(E) = σθ1(σθ2(E)). Commutativity of selection: σθ1(σθ2(E)) = σθ2(σθ1(E)). Cascade of projections: πL1(πL2(…πLn(E))) = πL1(E), if L1 ⊆ L2 ⊆ … ⊆ Ln. Selection with Cartesian product / join: σθ(E1 × E2) = E1 ⋈θ E2. Commutativity of join: E1 ⋈ E2 = E2 ⋈ E1. Associativity of natural join: (E1 ⋈ E2) ⋈ E3 = E1 ⋈ (E2 ⋈ E3). Selection distributes over join: if θ involves only attributes of E"}, {"t": "Relational Database design", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#relational-database-design", "x": ""}, {"t": "1) What are anomalies in database design? How can we solve it?", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#1-what-are-anomalies-in-database-design-how-can-we-solve-it", "x": "Anomalies are problems that occur in a relation because of redundancy (the same data stored many times). They appear while inserting, deleting or updating data. Consider EMP_DEPT, which stores employee and department data in a single table: Insertion anomaly: a new department (D3, IT, Joshi) cannot be added until it has at least one employee, because Emp_id (the key) cannot be NULL. Deletion anomaly: if Amit (the only employee of D2) is deleted, the information about department HR and its head Shah is lost as well. Update (modification) anomaly: if the head of Sales changes, the value Mehta must be changed in every row of Sales employees. If one row is missed, the data becomes inconsistent. "}, {"t": "2) What problems can occur due to wrong database design? How they can be solved?", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#2-what-problems-can-occur-due-to-wrong-database-design-how-they-can-be-solved", "x": "In general, a good design is obtained by applying functional dependencies and normal forms (1NF to BCNF) and checking that the decomposition is lossless."}, {"t": "3) Explain the issues of a database design.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#3-explain-the-issues-of-a-database-design", "x": "A bad (unnormalized) design creates the following issues, which the designer must avoid: Redundancy: repeated data increases storage and processing cost. Inconsistency: redundant copies may disagree after an update. Insertion, deletion and update anomalies (see Q1). Null values: attributes that do not apply to all tuples are stored as NULL. Integrity problems: hard to enforce keys and constraints correctly. Decomposition issues: when a large relation is split, we must ensure the split is lossless (original data can be reconstructed) and dependency preserving (all FDs can still be checked). R(Emp_id, Skill, Project) split into R1(Emp_id, Skill) and R2(Skill, Project) can produce wrong (spurio"}, {"t": "4) Explain BCNF with example.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#4-explain-bcnf-with-example", "x": "Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF) is a stricter version of 3NF. A relation R is in BCNF if, for every non-trivial functional dependency X → Y in R, X is a super key of R. In other words, every determinant must be a candidate (super) key. BCNF removes redundancy that remains in 3NF when a non-key attribute determines part of a candidate key (overlapping candidate keys). Relation TEACH(Student, Subject, Teacher) with these rules: a student can learn a subject from only one teacher, and each teacher teaches only one subject. FDs: (Student, Subject) → Teacher and Teacher → Subject. Candidate keys: (Student, Subject) and (Student, Teacher). The relation is in 3NF (all attributes are prime), but not i"}, {"t": "5) Explain how to find closure of a set of attributes?", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#5-explain-how-to-find-closure-of-a-set-of-attributes", "x": "The closure of a set of attributes X under a set of FDs F, written X+, is the set of all attributes that are functionally determined by X. It is used to find keys, to test whether an FD holds, and to check equivalence of FD sets. Start with result = X. Repeat: for each FD Y → Z in F, if Y ⊆ result, then add Z to result. Stop when no more attributes can be added. The final result is X+. R(A, B, C, D, E) with F = { A → B, BC → D, D → E }. Find (AC)+. (AC)+ = {A, B, C, D, E} = all attributes of R, hence AC is a super key (and a candidate key since neither A+ nor C+ is all of R). Testing an FD: X → Y holds in F if and only if Y ⊆ X+. Finding keys: X is a super key if X+ contains all attributes o"}, {"t": "6) Define functional dependency. Explain trivial and non-trivial FD with example.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#6-define-functional-dependency-explain-trivial-and-non-trivial-fd-with-example", "x": "A functional dependency (FD) X → Y between two sets of attributes of a relation R means that the value of X uniquely determines the value of Y: if two tuples have the same X value, they must have the same Y value. X is the determinant and Y the dependent. Example: Roll_no → Name (a roll number determines exactly one name). An FD X → Y is trivial if Y ⊆ X (the right side is a subset of the left side). It always holds. Examples: {Roll_no, Name} → Roll_no, and Name → Name. An FD X → Y is non-trivial if Y is not a subset of X (Y ⊄ X). It gives real constraints on the data. Example: Roll_no → Name. If X ∩ Y = ∅ as well (no common attributes) it is called a completely non-trivial FD."}, {"t": "7) Explain irreducible sets of functional dependency with example.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#7-explain-irreducible-sets-of-functional-dependency-with-example", "x": "An irreducible set of FDs (also called a minimal cover or canonical cover) is a set that is equivalent to the original set F, but has no redundancy. A set Fc is irreducible if: The right side of every FD has a single attribute. No FD has an extraneous attribute on the left side (removing any left attribute changes the closure). No FD is redundant (removing it changes the closure; it cannot be derived from the others). Split every FD so that the right side has one attribute. Remove extraneous attributes from left sides (for XA → B check whether X → B follows from F). Remove redundant FDs (an FD that can be derived from the remaining ones). Let F = { A → B, C → B, D → ABC, AC → D }. Single att"}, {"t": "8) What is functional dependency? Explain its usage in database design.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#8-what-is-functional-dependency-explain-its-usage-in-database-design", "x": "A functional dependency X → Y states that the value of attribute set X uniquely determines the value of attribute set Y (see Q6). It is a constraint derived from the meaning (semantics) of the data. Example: Emp_id → Emp_name, Dept_id → Dept_name. Finding keys: using closure, a set X is a candidate key if X+ is all attributes and X is minimal. Normalization: normal forms are defined with FDs: 2NF (no partial dependency), 3NF (no transitive dependency) and BCNF (every determinant is a super key). Removing redundancy and anomalies: FDs show which attributes should be kept in the same relation and which should be separated. Lossless decomposition: a split of R into R1 and R2 is lossless if (R1 "}, {"t": "9) What is Functional Dependency? Explain non-loss decomposition.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#9-what-is-functional-dependency-explain-non-loss-decomposition", "x": "Functional dependency: X → Y means each value of X is associated with exactly one value of Y (example: Dept_id → Dept_name). Non-loss (lossless-join) decomposition means that a relation R is split into two or more relations R1, R2, … in such a way that, when they are joined back by natural join, the original relation is obtained exactly, with no tuple lost and no extra (spurious) tuple added. That is, R1 ⋈ R2 = R. R1 ∪ R2 = R (all attributes are kept). R1 ∩ R2 ≠ ∅ (there is a common attribute). The common attributes form a key of at least one of the relations: (R1 ∩ R2) → R1 or (R1 ∩ R2) → R2. EMP(Emp_id, Name, Dept_id, Dept_name) with Dept_id → Dept_name. Decompose into R1(Emp_id, Name, Dep"}, {"t": "10) What is normalization? What is the need for normalization?", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#10-what-is-normalization-what-is-the-need-for-normalization", "x": "Normalization is the process of organising the attributes and tables of a relational database to reduce data redundancy and eliminate insertion, deletion and update anomalies. It is done by decomposing a large relation into smaller, well-structured relations using functional dependencies and a series of normal forms (1NF, 2NF, 3NF, BCNF, 4NF, 5NF). To minimise redundancy and save storage space. To avoid insertion, deletion and update anomalies. To maintain data consistency and integrity. To make the database design logical, simpler to understand and maintain. To make the structure flexible so that new data can be added without redesign. To make queries and updates on smaller tables faster an"}, {"t": "11) What is normalization? What is redundancy? Compare 1NF and 2NF with example.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#11-what-is-normalization-what-is-redundancy-compare-1nf-and-2nf-with-example", "x": "Normalization: the process of decomposing relations to remove redundancy and anomalies (see Q10). Redundancy: unnecessary repetition of the same data in many places in a database. It wastes space and may cause inconsistency. Example: writing the department name 'Sales' in every employee row of that department. A relation is in First Normal Form if all attributes contain only atomic (indivisible), single values: no multivalued attributes and no repeating groups. Example (not in 1NF): Converted to 1NF by giving each phone number its own row: A relation is in Second Normal Form if it is in 1NF and no non-prime attribute is partially dependent on any candidate key (every non-key attribute depend"}, {"t": "12) What is normalization? Explain 1NF, 2NF & 3NF.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#12-what-is-normalization-explain-1nf-2nf-3nf", "x": "Normalization is the process of decomposing relations into smaller relations to remove redundancy and anomalies, using functional dependencies and normal forms. Every attribute holds only atomic values; there are no multivalued attributes or repeating groups. Example: a Phone column holding '9811, 9822' violates 1NF; store each number in a separate row. The relation is in 1NF and every non-prime attribute is fully functionally dependent on every candidate key (no partial dependency). Example: ENROLL(Roll_no, Course_id, Student_name, Course_name, Grade) with key (Roll_no, Course_id): Roll_no → Student_name and Course_id → Course_name are partial dependencies. Decompose: STUDENT(Roll_no, Stude"}, {"t": "13) Why should normalization be performed on a table and what are its benefits. Explain 3NF and BCNF.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#13-why-should-normalization-be-performed-on-a-table-and-what-are-its-benefits-ex", "x": "Normalization should be performed to remove redundancy and the insertion, deletion and update anomalies that arise from a poorly designed table (see Q1, Q10). Less data redundancy and less storage space. Consistent, accurate data (a fact is stored and updated in only one place). No insertion, deletion or update anomalies. Better organised, understandable design with enforced integrity. Easier maintenance and extension of the database. Faster updates and index operations on smaller tables. A relation is in 3NF if it is in 2NF and no non-prime attribute is transitively dependent on the primary key. Equivalent: for every non-trivial FD X → A, either X is a super key or A is a prime attribute. E"}, {"t": "14) Explain 1NF, 2NF, 3NF and BCNF.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#14-explain-1nf-2nf-3nf-and-bcnf", "x": "Every BCNF relation is in 3NF, every 3NF relation is in 2NF, and every 2NF relation is in 1NF. Lossless decomposition into BCNF is always possible, but dependency preservation is guaranteed only up to 3NF."}, {"t": "15) Explain 2NF with example.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#15-explain-2nf-with-example", "x": "A relation is in Second Normal Form (2NF) if (i) it is in 1NF, and (ii) every non-prime attribute is fully functionally dependent on the whole candidate key; there is no partial dependency (a non-key attribute depending on only a part of a composite key). Example: ORDER_ITEM(Order_id, Product_id, Product_name, Quantity). Candidate key: (Order_id, Product_id). FDs: (Order_id, Product_id) → Quantity, but Product_id → Product_name is a partial dependency. 'Pen' is repeated in two rows, so there is redundancy and an update anomaly. Now every non-key attribute depends on the whole key of its table, and the product name is stored only once."}, {"t": "16) Explain 3NF with example.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#16-explain-3nf-with-example", "x": "A relation is in Third Normal Form (3NF) if it is in 2NF and there is no transitive dependency of a non-prime attribute on the primary key. For every non-trivial FD X → A, either X is a super key or A is a prime attribute. A transitive dependency exists when A → B and B → C, where B is not a key, so A → C indirectly. Example: EMPLOYEE(Emp_id, Emp_name, Dept_id, Dept_name). Key: Emp_id. FDs: Emp_id → Dept_id and Dept_id → Dept_name, therefore Emp_id → Dept_name is transitive. The department name 'Sales' is repeated; changing the name requires many updates. Both tables are in 3NF: no non-key attribute depends on another non-key attribute."}, {"t": "17) What is the advantage of using 3NF? Explain with example.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#17-what-is-the-advantage-of-using-3nf-explain-with-example", "x": "Removes transitive dependencies, so redundancy is greatly reduced. Avoids insertion, deletion and update anomalies caused by non-key dependencies. Always possible with a lossless-join and dependency-preserving decomposition (BCNF cannot always guarantee the latter). Good balance between a clean design and query performance (fewer joins than a highly decomposed schema). Easy to maintain and enforce integrity. Before 3NF: BOOK(ISBN, Title, Publisher, Publisher_city) with ISBN → Publisher and Publisher → Publisher_city. A publisher with 1000 books has its city stored 1000 times. Problems: Insert: a new publisher cannot be stored until a book exists. Delete: deleting the last book of a publisher"}, {"t": "18) Explain BCNF with example.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#18-explain-bcnf-with-example", "x": "A relation is in BCNF if for every non-trivial FD X → Y in it, X is a super key. It handles anomalies that remain in 3NF when a relation has several overlapping candidate keys. Example: COURSE_TEACH(Student, Course, Instructor). Rules: each instructor teaches one course, and a course can be taught by many instructors. FDs: (Student, Course) → Instructor and Instructor → Course. Candidate keys: (Student, Course), (Student, Instructor). It is in 3NF because Course is a prime attribute, but not in BCNF: in Instructor → Course the determinant Instructor is not a super key. The fact 'Shah teaches DBMS' is repeated for every student (redundancy). Each determinant is now a super key in its table; r"}, {"t": "19) Why do we need normalization? Explain 4NF & 5NF.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#19-why-do-we-need-normalization-explain-4nf-5nf", "x": "Need: to reduce redundancy, avoid insertion/deletion/update anomalies, keep data consistent and make the design easy to maintain (see Q10). The higher normal forms 4NF and 5NF deal with redundancy that comes from multivalued and join dependencies. A relation is in 4NF if it is in BCNF and it has no non-trivial multivalued dependency (MVD) except those where the determinant is a super key. An MVD X →→ Y means that for each value of X there is a set of Y values, independent of the other attributes. Example: STUDENT_INFO(Student, Course, Hobby). A student takes many courses and has many hobbies, and these two facts are independent: Student →→ Course and Student →→ Hobby. Every combination must "}, {"t": "20) What is non-loss decomposition in database? How it is useful in database?", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#20-what-is-non-loss-decomposition-in-database-how-it-is-useful-in-database", "x": "Non-loss (lossless-join) decomposition is the splitting of a relation R into smaller relations R1, R2, …, Rn so that the natural join of the parts gives back exactly the original relation: R1 ⋈ R2 ⋈ … ⋈ Rn = R (no tuple lost, no spurious tuple added). R1 ∪ R2 = R. R1 ∩ R2 ≠ ∅. (R1 ∩ R2) → R1 or (R1 ∩ R2) → R2 (the common attributes are a key of one part). R(Emp_id, Name, Dept_id, Dept_name) with Dept_id → Dept_name is split into R1(Emp_id, Name, Dept_id) and R2(Dept_id, Dept_name). Common attribute Dept_id → Dept_name (a key of R2), so the join gives back R: lossless. It allows a large relation to be normalized (redundancy and anomalies removed) without losing any information. The original r"}, {"t": "21) Normalize (decompose) the following relation into lower to higher normal form (from 1NF to 4NF).", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#21-normalize-decompose-the-following-relation-into-lower-to-higher-normal-form-f", "x": "Given relation PLANT (with repeating groups): Dependencies from the data: PLANT → MANAGER; SUPPLIER_NAME → SUPPLIER_CITY; and a plant has a set of machines and a set of suppliers that are independent of each other (PLANT →→ MACHINE | SUPPLIER_NAME). Each cell must hold a single value. Every machine is paired with every supplier of the plant (Plant-A: 2 × 2 = 4 rows, Plant-B: 3 × 3 = 9 rows). Key: (PLANT, MACHINE, SUPPLIER_NAME). Partial dependencies on the composite key: PLANT → MANAGER (part of key determines MANAGER) and SUPPLIER_NAME → SUPPLIER_CITY (part of key determines SUPPLIER_CITY). Remove them: In PLANT_MANAGER, SUPPLIER and PLANT_MACHINE_SUPPLIER there is no transitive dependency;"}, {"t": "22) What is canonical cover? Consider the following set F of functional dependencies on schema R(A, B, C) and compute the canonical cover for F. { A → BC, B → C, A → B, AB → C }", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#22-what-is-canonical-cover-consider-the-following-set-f-of-functional-dependenci", "x": "A canonical cover Fc of a set of FDs F is a minimal set that is equivalent to F (it has the same closure) and in which: (i) no FD contains an extraneous attribute, (ii) the left side of every FD is unique, and (iii) no FD is redundant. It simplifies checking and enforcement of constraints. (Also called a minimal / irreducible cover.) F = { A → BC, B → C, A → B, AB → C } Split the right sides: A → B, A → C, B → C, A → B (duplicate), AB → C. Remove the duplicate: { A → B, A → C, B → C, AB → C }. Remove extraneous attributes from the left side of AB → C: check whether A is extraneous. B → C is already in F, so (B)+ contains C. Hence A is extraneous and AB → C becomes B → C, which is already pre"}, {"t": "23) Consider a relation R with five attributes A, B, C, D, E having the following dependencies: A → B, BC → E and ED → A. (a) List all keys for R. (b) In which normal form is the table? Justify your answer.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#23-consider-a-relation-r-with-five-attributes-a-b-c-d-e-having-the-following-dep", "x": "Attributes that never appear on the right side of any FD must be part of every key. RHS attributes are B, E, A. So C and D must be in every key. (CD)+ = {C, D}. It does not give all attributes, so add one more attribute. (ACD)+: A → B gives B; then BC → E gives E. Result {A, B, C, D, E} = R. ACD is a key. (BCD)+: BC → E gives E; ED → A gives A. Result {A, B, C, D, E} = R. BCD is a key. (CDE)+: ED → A gives A; A → B gives B. Result = R. CDE is a key. Each of ACD, BCD and CDE is minimal (CD alone is not a key), and any other candidate key must contain one of them. Candidate keys: ACD, BCD, CDE. Prime attributes: A, B, C, D, E (every attribute is part of some key). 1NF: assumed (atomic attribut"}, {"t": "24) Given relation R with attributes A, B, C, D, E, F and set of FDs as A → BC, E → CF, B → E. Find out CD → EF. Find out closure {A, B}+ of the set of attributes.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#24-given-relation-r-with-attributes-a-b-c-d-e-f-and-set-of-fds-as-a-bc-e-cf-b-e-", "x": "{A, B}+ = {A, B, C, E, F}. D is not included, so AB is not a super key of R (the key of R is AD, since A+ = {A, B, C, E, F} and D must be added). Compute (CD)+: start with {C, D}. No FD has a left side contained in {C, D}: A → BC needs A, E → CF needs E, B → E needs B. So (CD)+ = {C, D}. Since E and F are not in (CD)+, the dependency CD → EF does not hold (it cannot be derived from the given set of FDs)."}, {"t": "25) Consider table R(A, B, C, D, E) with FDs as A → B, BC → E and ED → A. The table is in which normal form? Justify your answer.", "p": "Unit 2", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-2/#25-consider-table-ra-b-c-d-e-with-fds-as-a-b-bc-e-and-ed-a-the-table-is-in-which", "x": "Step 1: Find candidate keys. C and D never appear on the right side, so they are in every key. (ACD)+ = {A, B, C, D, E} (A → B, then BC → E). (BCD)+ = {A, B, C, D, E} (BC → E, ED → A). (CDE)+ = {A, B, C, D, E} (ED → A, A → B). Candidate keys: ACD, BCD, CDE; prime attributes: A, B, C, D, E. Step 2: Test the normal forms. Conclusion: The table R is in Third Normal Form (3NF) but not in BCNF. If BCNF is needed, decompose using A → B into R1(A, B) and R2(A, C, D, E)."}, {"t": "1) What is a transaction? Explain the ACID properties.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#1-what-is-a-transaction-explain-the-acid-properties", "x": "Transaction: A transaction is a logical unit of work consisting of a sequence of read and write operations that accesses and possibly updates data items in a database. It starts with BEGIN and ends with COMMIT or ROLLBACK. Example: transferring Rs. 500 from account A to account B. Read(A); A = A - 500; Write(A); Read(B); B = B + 500; Write(B); Atomicity (all or nothing): Either all operations of the transaction are executed or none are. If a failure occurs midway, all changes are undone (rolled back). Handled by the recovery manager. Consistency: A transaction takes the database from one consistent state to another consistent state. Integrity constraints must hold before and after (e.g., A +"}, {"t": "2) What is a transaction? Explain its four important properties.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#2-what-is-a-transaction-explain-its-four-important-properties", "x": "A transaction is a single logical unit of work, made of one or more SQL/read-write operations, that must be executed completely or not at all so that the database stays correct. The four important properties (ACID) are summarised below. Example: In a transfer of Rs. 500 from A to B, atomicity means both debit and credit happen or neither; consistency means A + B stays constant; isolation means another transaction cannot see A debited but B not yet credited; durability means after commit the transfer survives a crash. Active → Partially committed → Committed; Active/Partially committed → Failed → Aborted (rolled back)."}, {"t": "3) Why concurrency control is needed?", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#3-why-concurrency-control-is-needed", "x": "Concurrency control is the mechanism to manage simultaneous execution of transactions so that the database remains consistent. Running transactions concurrently improves throughput, CPU and disk utilisation, and reduces waiting and response time. But uncontrolled concurrent execution causes problems. Lost update problem: one transaction overwrites the update made by another. Dirty read (temporary update) problem: a transaction reads data written by an uncommitted transaction that later fails. Incorrect summary / unrepeatable read problem: a transaction sees different values or an inconsistent aggregate because other transactions update data meanwhile. These violate the Isolation and Consiste"}, {"t": "4) What is concurrency? What are the three problems due to concurrency? How the problems can be avoided, explain for one of the three problems.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#4-what-is-concurrency-what-are-the-three-problems-due-to-concurrency-how-the-pro", "x": "Concurrency means executing multiple transactions at the same time, with their operations interleaved, on a shared database. Two transactions read the same item and both update it; the update of one is lost. Let A = 100. Final A = 150 instead of 130. The update of T1 is lost. T1 updates a value and later fails/rolls back, but T2 has already read that uncommitted value and used it, so T2 works with data that never existed. T1 calculates an aggregate (e.g., SUM of balances) while T2 is updating some of those values. T1 may count some values before and some after the update, giving a wrong total. In the unrepeatable-read form, T1 reads the same item twice and gets different values because T2 mo"}, {"t": "5) Consider schedule S with transaction T1 and T2. T1 transfer Rs. 150 from account A to C and T2 adds Rs. 50 into account A. Prepare concurrent schedule with two phase locking protocol.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#5-consider-schedule-s-with-transaction-t1-and-t2-t1-transfer-rs-150-from-account", "x": "Transactions: T1: Read(A); A = A - 150; Write(A); Read(C); C = C + 150; Write(C) T2: Read(A); A = A + 50; Write(A) Assume initial A = 500, C = 300. Both transactions use the exclusive lock (Lock-X) and follow 2PL: all locks are acquired (growing phase) before any lock is released (shrinking phase). Result: A = 400, C = 450 (total A + C = 850, unchanged except the Rs. 50 added by T2). The schedule is conflict serializable and equivalent to the serial schedule T1 then T2. T1 holds A until it has also locked C, so T2 cannot see an inconsistent state."}, {"t": "6) Explain briefly the meaning of serializability of transactions.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#6-explain-briefly-the-meaning-of-serializability-of-transactions", "x": "A schedule is the order in which operations of concurrent transactions are executed. A serial schedule runs one transaction completely before the next; it is always correct but slow. A serializable schedule is a concurrent (interleaved) schedule whose effect is the same as that of some serial schedule of the same transactions. Serializability is the accepted criterion of correctness for concurrent execution. It guarantees Isolation and database consistency while permitting concurrency. With n transactions there are n! possible serial schedules; a schedule is serializable if equivalent to any one of them. Two forms: conflict serializability and view serializability."}, {"t": "7) Explain both the forms of serializability & relation between the two.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#7-explain-both-the-forms-of-serializability-relation-between-the-two", "x": "Two instructions of different transactions conflict if they access the same data item and at least one is a Write (R-W, W-R, W-W). A schedule S is conflict serializable if it can be converted into a serial schedule by swapping adjacent non-conflicting operations (conflict equivalence). It is tested with a precedence graph: the schedule is conflict serializable iff the graph has no cycle. Schedules S and S' are view equivalent if: (i) for each item Q, the transaction that reads the initial value of Q is the same in both; (ii) for each read, the transaction whose write is read (Ti reads Q written by Tj) is the same; (iii) the transaction doing the final write on each item Q is the same. A sche"}, {"t": "8) Explain conflict serializability with example.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#8-explain-conflict-serializability-with-example", "x": "A schedule is conflict serializable if it is conflict equivalent to a serial schedule, i.e., it can be obtained from a serial schedule by swapping adjacent non-conflicting operations. Conflicting pairs (same item, different transactions, at least one write): Ri(Q)-Wj(Q), Wi(Q)-Rj(Q), Wi(Q)-Wj(Q). Read-Read never conflicts. Create a node for each transaction. Draw edge Ti → Tj if an operation of Ti conflicts with a later operation of Tj. If the graph has a cycle, the schedule is not conflict serializable; otherwise it is, and a topological order gives the equivalent serial schedule. Conflicts: W1(A) before R2(A) and W2(A) gives edge T1 → T2; W1(B) before R2(B) and W2(B) gives edge T1 → T2. On"}, {"t": "9) Explain view serializability with example.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#9-explain-view-serializability-with-example", "x": "Schedule S is view serializable if it is view equivalent to some serial schedule. Schedules S1 and S2 are view equivalent when all three conditions hold for every data item Q: Initial read: if Ti reads the initial value of Q in S1, it also does in S2. Read-from (updated read): if Ti reads a value of Q written by Tj in S1, the same happens in S2. Final write: the transaction that performs the last Write(Q) in S1 also does it in S2. This schedule is not conflict serializable (R1(A)-W2(A) gives T1 → T2; W2(A)-W1(A) gives T2 → T1: a cycle). Check against the serial schedule T1, T2, T3: T1 reads the initial value of A in both; no transaction reads a value written by another; the final write of A "}, {"t": "10) What is deadlock? When it is occurs and how to avoid it?", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#10-what-is-deadlock-when-it-is-occurs-and-how-to-avoid-it", "x": "Deadlock is a situation in which two or more transactions are each waiting for a lock held by another transaction in the set, so none of them can proceed. Example: T1 holds Lock-X(A) and requests B; T2 holds Lock-X(B) and requests A. Both wait forever. Mutual exclusion: a resource is held in exclusive mode. Hold and wait: a transaction holds some locks while waiting for others. No pre-emption: locks cannot be forcibly taken away. Circular wait: a cycle of transactions each waiting for the next. Conservative 2PL: a transaction acquires all its locks before it starts (no hold-and-wait). Ordering of data items: locks must be requested in a fixed global order. Wait-Die and Wound-Wait timestamp s"}, {"t": "11) What is deadlock? Explain Wait-For-Graph.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#11-what-is-deadlock-explain-wait-for-graph", "x": "Deadlock: a state where every transaction in a set is waiting for a resource (lock) held by another transaction in the same set, so none can continue (see Q10). A wait-for graph is a directed graph G = (V, E) used to detect deadlock. Vertices are the active transactions. An edge Ti → Tj exists if Ti is waiting for a data item locked by Tj. The edge is removed when Tj releases the lock. If the graph contains a cycle, a deadlock exists, and every transaction in the cycle is deadlocked. The system checks the graph periodically. Here T1 waits for T2, T2 waits for T3 and T3 waits for T1, forming the cycle T1 → T2 → T3 → T1, hence deadlock. Without a cycle (e.g., only T1 → T2 → T3) there is no dea"}, {"t": "12) Explain deadlock detection mechanism.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#12-explain-deadlock-detection-mechanism", "x": "In the detection approach the system allows deadlocks to occur, then detects them and recovers. It consists of three steps: Create a node per transaction. When Ti requests an item held by Tj, add edge Ti → Tj; remove it when Tj releases the lock. The system invokes a cycle detection algorithm (e.g., depth-first search) periodically, or whenever a lock request has waited longer than a threshold. A cycle means deadlock. Frequency depends on how often deadlocks occur and how many transactions are affected. Victim selection: choose one transaction in the cycle to abort, using least cost: smallest work done, fewest locks held, fewest items still to access, least number of times already rolled bac"}, {"t": "13) Explain various deadlock prevention methods.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#13-explain-various-deadlock-prevention-methods", "x": "Prevention ensures that the system never enters a deadlock by breaking at least one of the necessary conditions. A transaction requests all required locks before it begins; if any is unavailable it waits holding none. Breaks hold and wait. Drawbacks: hard to predict needed items, low concurrency and possible starvation. Impose a total order on all data items; transactions must lock items only in increasing order. This prevents circular wait. Each transaction gets a timestamp TS on start (older = smaller TS). Ti requests an item held by Tj: Both schemes avoid deadlock and starvation because a restarted transaction keeps its original timestamp, but may cause unnecessary rollbacks. A transactio"}, {"t": "14) Explain Two-Phase Locking protocol.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#14-explain-two-phase-locking-protocol", "x": "Two-Phase Locking (2PL) is a concurrency control protocol that guarantees conflict serializability. Each transaction issues lock and unlock requests in two distinct phases: Growing phase: the transaction may obtain locks but may not release any lock. Shrinking phase: the transaction may release locks but may not obtain any new lock. The point where the last lock is acquired is the lock point; transactions are serialized in the order of their lock points. Lock modes: Shared (S) for read; Exclusive (X) for write. Compatibility: S-S allowed; S-X, X-S and X-X not allowed. Advantages: ensures serializability. Disadvantages: does not prevent deadlock; may cause cascading rollbacks (a transaction r"}, {"t": "15) Explain Strict two phase locking with advantages & disadvantages.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#15-explain-strict-two-phase-locking-with-advantages-disadvantages", "x": "Strict 2PL is a variant of 2PL in which a transaction holds all its exclusive (write) locks until it commits or aborts. Locks are released only at the end of the transaction, so no other transaction can read or write uncommitted data. (Rigorous 2PL holds both shared and exclusive locks until commit.) Guarantees conflict serializability. Cascadeless schedules: no cascading rollbacks, since uncommitted data is never read. Ensures recoverable schedules; simple to implement. Easy recovery because aborting a transaction affects no others. Deadlock is still possible. Reduced concurrency because locks are held longer, so other transactions wait. Lower throughput and longer response time for long tr"}, {"t": "16) Define Locking. Explain two phase locking protocol.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#16-define-locking-explain-two-phase-locking-protocol", "x": "Locking is a mechanism in which a transaction must obtain a lock on a data item before accessing it. A lock is a variable associated with a data item that controls concurrent access to it, so that conflicting operations do not execute at the same time. Operations: Lock-S(Q), Lock-X(Q), Unlock(Q). If the lock cannot be granted, the transaction waits. 2PL requires every transaction to lock and unlock in two phases: a growing phase (acquires locks, releases none) followed by a shrinking phase (releases locks, acquires none). Once a transaction releases its first lock it cannot request any new lock. Every schedule produced by 2PL is conflict serializable, serialised in lock point order. Limitati"}, {"t": "17) Explain Locked based protocol.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#17-explain-locked-based-protocol", "x": "A lock-based protocol controls concurrent access by requiring each transaction to acquire a suitable lock on a data item before reading or writing it. Access is permitted only according to a lock compatibility matrix. Lock-S(Q): shared lock; the transaction can only read Q. Lock-X(Q): exclusive lock; the transaction can read and write Q. Unlock(Q): releases the lock. A transaction waits if the requested lock conflicts with locks held by others. Simply locking is not enough: releasing locks too early can still produce non-serializable results. Therefore a locking protocol (set of rules for when to lock and unlock) is used. Common protocols: Two-Phase Locking (2PL): growing and shrinking phase"}, {"t": "18) Explain Two phase commit protocol.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#18-explain-two-phase-commit-protocol", "x": "The Two-Phase Commit (2PC) protocol is used in distributed databases to ensure that a transaction that runs at several sites either commits at all sites or aborts at all sites (atomicity). One site acts as the coordinator; the other sites are participants (cohorts). Coordinator writes <prepare T> in its log and sends PREPARE message to all participants. Each participant checks whether it can commit. If yes, it writes <ready T> to its log, force-writes the log to disk and replies READY (Yes vote). If it cannot commit, it writes <no T> and replies NOT READY (No vote) and aborts locally. If all participants voted READY, the coordinator writes <commit T> and sends COMMIT to all; participants com"}, {"t": "19) What is System recovery? Explain two phase commit protocol.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#19-what-is-system-recovery-explain-two-phase-commit-protocol", "x": "System recovery is the process of restoring the database to the latest correct (consistent) state after a failure such as a transaction failure, system crash (loss of main memory) or disk failure. The recovery manager guarantees atomicity and durability using logs, checkpoints, shadow paging and backups: changes of committed transactions are redone and changes of uncommitted transactions are undone. 2PC is the atomic commit protocol for distributed transactions, using a coordinator and participants: Phase 1 (Prepare / voting): coordinator sends PREPARE; each participant force-writes <ready T> to its log and votes READY, or votes NO and aborts. Phase 2 (Commit / abort): if all voted READY, th"}, {"t": "20) Explain immediate database modification log based recovery method. Also explain role of check point in log base.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#20-explain-immediate-database-modification-log-based-recovery-method-also-explai", "x": "A log is a sequence of records describing all update activities. Log records: <Ti start>, <Ti, X, V1, V2> (transaction, data item, old value, new value), <Ti commit>, <Ti abort>. The log is written to stable storage before the database is modified (write-ahead logging). Updates are applied directly to the database buffer/disk while the transaction is still active (before commit), after the corresponding log record has been written. Because uncommitted changes may reach the database, the log must store both the old value (for undo) and the new value (for redo). Undo(Ti): restore all items updated by Ti to their old values, going backward in the log. Required if the log has <Ti start> but no <"}, {"t": "21) Explain log based recovery and mention all its types.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#21-explain-log-based-recovery-and-mention-all-its-types", "x": "Log-based recovery keeps a log (journal) on stable storage that records every update. Before a change is made to the database, its log record is written first (write-ahead logging). After a failure, the log is used to redo committed transactions and undo uncommitted ones, thereby preserving atomicity and durability. <Ti start> <Ti, X, V1, V2> : Ti wrote item X, old value V1, new value V2 <Ti commit> and <Ti abort> Techniques to improve both types: checkpoints to limit the part of the log examined. (Shadow paging is a log-free alternative.)"}, {"t": "22) Explain system recovery procedure with check point record concept.", "p": "Unit 3", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-3/#22-explain-system-recovery-procedure-with-check-point-record-concept", "x": "When the system restarts after a crash, the recovery manager uses the log and the latest checkpoint to bring the database back to a consistent state. A <checkpoint L> record is written periodically after flushing the log buffer and all modified buffer blocks to disk, where L is the list of transactions active at the time. It marks a point up to which the database on disk is known to be up to date. Step 1: Scan the log backward to find the most recent <checkpoint L> record. Step 2: Initialise undo-list = L (the transactions active at the checkpoint) and redo-list = empty. Step 3: Scan forward from the checkpoint. For each <Ti start> add Ti to the undo-list. For each <Ti commit> move Ti from t"}, {"t": "Part A: SQL Concepts", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#part-a-sql-concepts", "x": ""}, {"t": "1) Define: (1) Primary key (2) Foreign key (3) Unique Key (4) Not null (5) Commit (6) Candidate key (7) Rollback.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#1-define-1-primary-key-2-foreign-key-3-unique-key-4-not-null-5-commit-6-candidat", "x": "Primary key: A column (or set of columns) that uniquely identifies each row of a table. It cannot contain NULL or duplicate values and a table has only one primary key. Example: rollno INT PRIMARY KEY. Foreign key: A column in one table that refers to the primary key (or unique key) of another table. It enforces referential integrity, so the value must exist in the parent table or be NULL. Example: dept_id REFERENCES Dept(dept_id). Unique key: A constraint that ensures all values in a column are different. Unlike a primary key it allows NULL (in most DBMS) and a table can have many unique keys. Example: email VARCHAR(50) UNIQUE. Not null: A constraint that does not allow a column to hold NUL"}, {"t": "2) Define: (1) Weak entity (2) Data Dictionary (3) Substring( ) (4) Dual (5) Alter (6) Truncate (7) Drop.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#2-define-1-weak-entity-2-data-dictionary-3-substring-4-dual-5-alter-6-truncate-7", "x": "Weak entity: An entity that does not have enough attributes to form a primary key of its own and depends on a strong (owner) entity through an identifying relationship. It has a partial key (discriminator) and is shown by a double rectangle in the ER diagram. Example: Dependent of an Employee. Data dictionary: A set of system-maintained tables that store metadata (data about data): table names, columns, data types, constraints, users, privileges, indexes and views. Example in Oracle: USER_TABLES, USER_TAB_COLUMNS. Substring( ): A string function that extracts part of a string from a given starting position for a given length. Syntax: SUBSTR(string, start, length). Example: SUBSTR('DATABASE',"}, {"t": "3) What is ON DELETE CASCADE in SQL? Explain clearly with example.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#3-what-is-on-delete-cascade-in-sql-explain-clearly-with-example", "x": "ON DELETE CASCADE is an option of a FOREIGN KEY constraint. When a row in the parent table is deleted, all the matching rows in the child table that refer to it through the foreign key are automatically deleted. It keeps referential integrity without deleting the child rows manually. Without it, deleting a parent row that is still referenced gives an error. CREATE TABLE Dept ( dept_id INT PRIMARY KEY, dept_name VARCHAR(20) ); CREATE TABLE Emp ( emp_id INT PRIMARY KEY, emp_name VARCHAR(20), dept_id INT, FOREIGN KEY (dept_id) REFERENCES De DELETE FROM Dept WHERE dept_id = 10; Result: Dept row 10 is deleted and Emp rows Ravi and Meena (dept_id = 10) are deleted automatically. Only John remains."}, {"t": "4) What is a join? Explain various types of joins with example.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#4-what-is-a-join-explain-various-types-of-joins-with-example", "x": "A join combines rows from two or more tables based on a related column (usually primary key and foreign key) so that data stored in separate tables can be retrieved together in one result. Sample tables used for all examples: Returns only rows that have matching values in both tables. SELECT e.ename, d.dname FROM Emp e INNER JOIN Dept d ON e.did = d.did; Returns all rows of the left table and matching rows of the right table; unmatched right values are NULL. SELECT e.ename, d.dname FROM Emp e LEFT OUTER JOIN Dept d ON e.did = d.did; Returns all rows of the right table and matching rows of the left table; unmatched left values are NULL. SELECT e.ename, d.dname FROM Emp e RIGHT OUTER JOIN Dept"}, {"t": "5) Explain natural join operation with example.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#5-explain-natural-join-operation-with-example", "x": "A natural join joins two tables automatically on all columns with the same name and compatible data type. No ON condition is written, and the common column appears only once in the result. It is equal to an inner join on the common columns followed by removal of the duplicate column. In relational algebra it is denoted by the natural join symbol between R and S. Syntax: SELECT * FROM table1 NATURAL JOIN table2; SELECT * FROM Student NATURAL JOIN Dept; Common column did is used for matching and is shown once. Karan (did 30) and Mech (did 40) have no match, so they are not in the output. Caution: if the tables share other unintended column names, they will also be used for joining."}, {"t": "6) Explain DDL, DML, DCL with example.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#6-explain-ddl-dml-dcl-with-example", "x": "CREATE TABLE Student (rollno INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(30), city VARCHAR(20)); ALTER TABLE Student ADD age INT; TRUNCATE TABLE Student; DROP TABLE Student; INSERT INTO Student VALUES (1, 'Amit', 'Surat', 20); UPDATE Student SET city = 'Rajkot' WHERE rollno = 1; DELETE FROM Student WHERE rollno = 1; SELECT * FROM Student; GRANT SELECT, INSERT ON Student TO user1; REVOKE INSERT ON Student FROM user1; TCL (Transaction Control Language): COMMIT, ROLLBACK and SAVEPOINT control transactions made by DML statements."}, {"t": "7) Write with example various built in string functions.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#7-write-with-example-various-built-in-string-functions", "x": "SELECT UPPER(ename), LENGTH(ename) FROM Emp; SELECT 'Data' || 'base' FROM DUAL;"}, {"t": "8) Explain any two string functions in SQL.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#8-explain-any-two-string-functions-in-sql", "x": "Converts all characters of a string to uppercase. Syntax: UPPER(string) SELECT UPPER('database management') FROM DUAL; -- Result: DATABASE MANAGEMENT SELECT UPPER(sname) FROM Supplier; Extracts a part of a string. Syntax: SUBSTR(string, start_position, length). The first position is 1; a negative start counts from the end (Oracle); if length is omitted, the rest of the string is returned. SELECT SUBSTR('DATABASE', 1, 4) FROM DUAL; -- DATA SELECT SUBSTR('DATABASE', 5) FROM DUAL; -- BASE SELECT SUBSTR('DATABASE', -4, 2) FROM DUAL; -- BA"}, {"t": "9) Explain any two aggregate functions of SQL.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#9-explain-any-two-aggregate-functions-of-sql", "x": "Aggregate functions work on a set of rows and return a single value. They ignore NULL values (except COUNT(*)) and are often used with GROUP BY. Other aggregate functions: COUNT, MAX, MIN. Returns the total of the values of a numeric column. Syntax: SUM([DISTINCT] column) SELECT SUM(sal) FROM Emp; -- 50000 (NULL ignored) Returns the average of the non-NULL values of a numeric column. Syntax: AVG(column) SELECT AVG(sal) FROM Emp; -- 25000 (50000 / 2 values) With grouping: SELECT deptno, SUM(sal), AVG(sal) FROM Emp GROUP BY deptno;"}, {"t": "10) What is View? Give the advantages of View.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#10-what-is-view-give-the-advantages-of-view", "x": "A view is a virtual table based on the result of a stored SELECT query. It does not store data itself; it displays data from one or more base tables and is recomputed every time it is used. CREATE VIEW emp_dept AS SELECT e.ename, e.sal, d.dname FROM Emp e JOIN Dept d ON e.deptno = d.deptno WHERE e.sal > 3000; SELECT * FROM emp_dept; DROP VIEW emp_dept; Security: hides sensitive columns/rows; users see only permitted data. Simplicity: hides complex joins and queries behind a simple name. Logical data independence: the base table structure can change without changing applications. Customised presentation: different users get different views of the same data. Consistency and reuse: one query de"}, {"t": "11) Explain what is NULL?", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#11-explain-what-is-null", "x": "NULL is a special marker that represents a value that is missing, unknown or not applicable. It is not the same as zero or an empty string. A column can hold NULL unless it has a NOT NULL or PRIMARY KEY constraint. Comparison with NULL using =, <> gives UNKNOWN, so use IS NULL or IS NOT NULL. Any arithmetic with NULL gives NULL (e.g., 100 + NULL = NULL). Aggregate functions (SUM, AVG, COUNT(column)) ignore NULLs. Logic is three-valued: TRUE, FALSE, UNKNOWN. NULL = NULL is UNKNOWN, not TRUE. Functions to handle NULL: NVL(x, 0) in Oracle, COALESCE(x, 0). SELECT ename FROM Emp WHERE comm IS NULL; SELECT ename, sal + NVL(comm, 0) FROM Emp;"}, {"t": "12) Supplier (S#, sname, status, city), Parts (P#, pname, color, weight, city), SP (S#, P#, quantity). Write SQL queries.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#12-supplier-s-sname-status-city-parts-p-pname-color-weight-city-sp-s-p-quantity-", "x": "Oracle allows # in names. If your DBMS does not, rename S# and P# to S_no and P_no. SELECT sname FROM Supplier WHERE city = 'Delhi'; SELECT * FROM Supplier WHERE sname LIKE 'AB%'; SELECT * FROM Supplier WHERE status IN (10, 20, 30); SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT city) FROM Supplier; -- number of different cities SELECT DISTINCT sp.S# FROM SP sp JOIN Parts p ON sp.P# = p.P# WHERE p.color = 'red'; SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT sp.S#) FROM SP sp JOIN Parts p ON sp.P# = p.P# WHERE p.color = 'red'; SELECT * FROM Supplier ORDER BY sname; DELETE FROM Supplier WHERE status = 40; If SP has a foreign key to Supplier, first delete the related SP rows or define ON DELETE CASCADE. ALTER TABLE Supplier ADD phone VARCHAR("}, {"t": "13) Student (rollno, name, branch), Exam (rollno, subject_code, obtained_marks, paper_code), Papers (paper_code, paper_setter_name, university). Write SQL.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#13-student-rollno-name-branch-exam-rollno-subjectcode-obtainedmarks-papercode-pa", "x": "Assumption: first class means obtained marks of 60 or more (out of 100). SELECT DISTINCT s.name FROM Student s JOIN Exam e ON s.rollno = e.rollno WHERE e.subject_code = '130703' AND e.obtained_marks >= 60; SELECT s.name, SUM(e.obtained_marks) AS total_marks FROM Student s JOIN Exam e ON s.rollno = e.rollno GROUP BY s.rollno, s.name; SELECT p.university, COUNT(DISTINCT e.rollno) AS no_of_students FROM Exam e JOIN Papers p ON e.paper_code = p.paper_code GROUP BY p.university; SELECT s.name FROM Student s WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM Exam e WHERE e.rollno = s.rollno);"}, {"t": "14) Person (ss#, name, address), Car (license, year, model), Accident (date, driver, damage-amount), Owns (ss#, license), Log (license, date, driver). Write queries.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#14-person-ss-name-address-car-license-year-model-accident-date-driver-damage-amo", "x": "Assumptions: Accident.driver and Log.driver hold the ss# of the driver; each Log row records one car involved in the accident on that date. If DATE is reserved in your DBMS, write it as \"date\" or rename it accident_date. Hyphens are written as underscores. SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT o.ss#) FROM Owns o JOIN Log l ON o.license = l.license WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM l.date) = 2009; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Person p JOIN Owns o ON p.ss# = o.ss# JOIN Log l ON o.license = l.license WHERE p.name = 'S.Sudarshan'; INSERT INTO Person (ss#, name, address) VALUES (1005, 'Rahul Shah', 'Ahmedabad'); INSERT INTO Accident (date, driver, damage_amount) SELECT DATE '2026-11-20', p.ss#, 15000 FROM Person p WHERE p.name = "}, {"t": "15) Employee (employee-name, street, city), Works (employee-name, company-name, salary), Company (company-name, city), Manages (employee-name, manager-name). Write SQL.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#15-employee-employee-name-street-city-works-employee-name-company-name-salary-co", "x": "Hyphens in column names are written as underscores. SELECT employee_name FROM Works WHERE company_name = 'State Bank'; SELECT e.employee_name, e.city FROM Employee e JOIN Works w ON e.employee_name = w.employee_name WHERE w.company_name = 'State Bank'; SELECT employee_name FROM Employee WHERE employee_name NOT IN (SELECT employee_name FROM Works WHERE company_name = 'State Bank'); SELECT employee_name FROM Works WHERE salary > ALL (SELECT salary FROM Works WHERE company_name = 'UCO Bank');"}, {"t": "16) Student (Rollno, Name, Age, Sex, City), Student_marks (Rollno, Sub1, Sub2, Sub3, Total, Average). Write queries.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#16-student-rollno-name-age-sex-city-studentmarks-rollno-sub1-sub2-sub3-total-ave", "x": "UPDATE Student_marks SET Total = Sub1 + Sub2 + Sub3, Average = (Sub1 + Sub2 + Sub3) / 3; COMMIT; SELECT s.Name FROM Student s JOIN Student_marks m ON s.Rollno = m.Rollno WHERE m.Sub1 > 60; SELECT s.Name, m.Total, m.Average FROM Student s JOIN Student_marks m ON s.Rollno = m.Rollno; SELECT s.Name, m.Sub2 FROM Student s JOIN Student_marks m ON s.Rollno = m.Rollno WHERE m.Sub2 IN (SELECT Sub2 FROM Student_marks GROUP BY Sub2 HAVING COUNT(*) > 1) ORDER BY m.Sub2;"}, {"t": "17) Student (stud_no, stud_name, sub1, sub2, totalmark, percentage). Implement using SQL.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#17-student-studno-studname-sub1-sub2-totalmark-percentage-implement-using-sql", "x": "Assumption: two subjects, each out of 100, so percentage = total / 2. CREATE TABLE Student ( stud_no NUMBER(5) PRIMARY KEY, stud_name VARCHAR2(30) NOT NULL, sub1 NUMBER(3), sub2 NUMBER(3), totalmark NUMBER(4), percentage NUMBER(5,2) ); INSER UPDATE Student SET totalmark = sub1 + sub2, percentage = (sub1 + sub2) / 2; SELECT * FROM Student ORDER BY totalmark ASC; CREATE VIEW student_rank AS SELECT stud_no, stud_name, sub1, sub2, totalmark UPDATE Student SET sub1 = 50, totalmark = 50 + sub2, percentage = (50 + sub2) / 2 WHERE stud_no = 111; COMMIT; Result for student 111: sub1 = 50, sub2 = 60, totalmark = 110, percentage = 55. (In an UPDATE, the right side uses old column values, so the new val"}, {"t": "18) Employee (emp_no, emp_name, department, city, salary). Implement using SQL.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#18-employee-empno-empname-department-city-salary-implement-using-sql", "x": "SELECT * FROM Employee WHERE emp_no < 100 AND salary > 25000 AND department = 'Account'; SELECT COUNT(*) AS total_employees, SUM(salary) AS total_salary FROM Employee; DELETE FROM Employee WHERE salary = (SELECT MIN(salary) FROM Employee);"}, {"t": "19) EMP (empno, ename, jobtitle, managerno, hiredate, sal, comm, deptno), DEPT (deptno, dname, loc). Answer in SQL.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#19-emp-empno-ename-jobtitle-managerno-hiredate-sal-comm-deptno-dept-deptno-dname", "x": "SELECT * FROM EMP WHERE sal > 3000 AND deptno = 20; SELECT * FROM EMP WHERE comm IS NULL; -- or: comm IS NULL OR comm = 0 SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT jobtitle) FROM EMP; SELECT jobtitle, SUM(sal) AS total_salary FROM EMP GROUP BY jobtitle; SELECT d.dname, COUNT(e.empno) AS no_of_employees FROM DEPT d LEFT JOIN EMP e ON d.deptno = e.deptno GROUP BY d.deptno, d.dname; SELECT ename FROM EMP WHERE managerno IS NULL; SELECT ename, sal FROM EMP WHERE sal BETWEEN 1500 AND 3500;"}, {"t": "20) Using EMP and DEPT tables of Q19, answer the following in SQL.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#20-using-emp-and-dept-tables-of-q19-answer-the-following-in-sql", "x": "SELECT * FROM EMP WHERE deptno IN (10, 20, 30); SELECT * FROM EMP WHERE ename LIKE 'A%' OR ename LIKE 'a%'; -- or: WHERE UPPER(ename) LIKE 'A%' SELECT e.ename, d.dname FROM EMP e JOIN DEPT d ON e.deptno = d.deptno; SELECT ename FROM EMP WHERE managerno = (SELECT empno FROM EMP WHERE ename = 'KING'); SELECT ename FROM EMP WHERE deptno = (SELECT deptno FROM EMP WHERE ename = 'SMITH'); SELECT ename, sal FROM EMP WHERE sal > (SELECT sal FROM EMP WHERE ename = 'ALLEN'); SELECT ename, deptno, sal FROM EMP e WHERE sal = (SELECT MAX(sal) FROM EMP WHERE deptno = e.deptno);"}, {"t": "21) T1 (Empno, Ename, Salary, Designation), T2 (Empno, Deptno). Write queries.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#21-t1-empno-ename-salary-designation-t2-empno-deptno-write-queries", "x": "SELECT * FROM T1 WHERE Salary > 5000; SELECT t2.Deptno FROM T1 t1 JOIN T2 t2 ON t1.Empno = t2.Empno WHERE t1.Ename = 'syham'; T2 already has Deptno, so the new column needed for part (vi) is the department name. Adding it: ALTER TABLE T2 ADD Deptname VARCHAR(20); UPDATE T2 SET Deptname = 'Sales' WHERE Deptno = 10; If T2 really lacked Deptno, the statement would be ALTER TABLE T2 ADD Deptno NUMBER(3); UPDATE T1 SET Designation = 'senior clerk' WHERE Ename = 'ram' AND Designation = 'clerk'; SELECT SUM(Salary) FROM T1; SELECT t1.Empno, t1.Ename, t2.Deptno, t2.Deptname FROM T1 t1 JOIN T2 t2 ON t1.Empno = t2.Empno; DROP TABLE T1;"}, {"t": "22) Student (RollNo, Name, Age, Sex, City), Student_marks (RollNo, Sub1, Sub2, Sub3, Total, Average). Write SQL.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#22-student-rollno-name-age-sex-city-studentmarks-rollno-sub1-sub2-sub3-total-ave", "x": "SELECT s.Name, s.City FROM Student s JOIN Student_marks m ON s.RollNo = m.RollNo WHERE m.Total > 225; SELECT s.Name FROM Student s JOIN Student_marks m ON s.RollNo = m.RollNo WHERE m.Sub1 > 60 AND m.Sub2 > 60 AND m.Sub3 > 60; SELECT City FROM Student GROUP BY City HAVING COUNT(*) > 10; SELECT m.Name AS male_student, f.Name AS female_student FROM Student m, Student f WHERE m.Sex = 'M' AND f.Sex = 'F'; A self join pairs each male student with each female student exactly once. Add AND m.City = f.City to pair students of the same city."}, {"t": "Part B: PL/SQL Concepts", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#part-b-plsql-concepts", "x": ""}, {"t": "1) Explain the advantages of PL/SQL.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#1-explain-the-advantages-of-plsql", "x": "PL/SQL (Procedural Language extension to SQL) is Oracle's block-structured language that combines SQL with procedural features such as variables, conditions, loops and exception handling. Its main advantages are: Tight integration with SQL: SQL statements and data types can be used directly inside the program. Procedural constructs: IF-ELSE, CASE, LOOP, WHILE and FOR give full programming control, which SQL alone lacks. Better performance: a whole block is sent to the server in one call, which reduces network traffic and execution time. Modularity and reusability: programs are divided into blocks, procedures, functions and packages that are stored and reused. Exception handling: run-time err"}, {"t": "2) Explain commit and rollback command.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#2-explain-commit-and-rollback-command", "x": "Both are TCL (Transaction Control Language) commands that manage the changes made by DML statements. INSERT INTO Dept VALUES (50, 'Audit', 'Pune'); SAVEPOINT sp1; UPDATE Emp SET sal = sal + 1000 WHERE deptno = 10; ROLLBACK TO sp1; -- undoes only the UPDATE COMMIT; -- saves the INSERT SAVEPOINT marks a point within a transaction to which a partial rollback can be done. DDL commands (CREATE, ALTER, DROP) and normal exit from SQL*Plus cause an implicit commit. On a system failure or abnormal termination, uncommitted work is automatically rolled back. Together they ensure the Atomicity and Durability of transactions."}, {"t": "3) Write note on Cursor and its types.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#3-write-note-on-cursor-and-its-types", "x": "A cursor is a pointer to a private SQL work area (context area) in memory where Oracle stores the result of a SQL statement. It allows PL/SQL to process the rows of a query result one by one. There are two types: Created automatically by Oracle for every INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE and SELECT ... INTO statement. The programmer cannot control it, but its status is available through the cursor named SQL. BEGIN UPDATE emp SET sal = sal + 500 WHERE deptno = 10; IF SQL%FOUND THEN DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE(SQL%ROWCOUNT || ' rows updated'); END IF; END; / Declared and controlled by the programmer for queries that return more than one row. Four steps: DECLARE (define the cursor with its query), OPEN (execute "}, {"t": "4) Write short note on database triggers in PL/SQL.", "p": "Unit 4", "u": "sem-3/question-banks/dbms/unit-4/#4-write-short-note-on-database-triggers-in-plsql", "x": "A database trigger is a stored PL/SQL block that is executed automatically (fired) by the database when a specified event occurs on a table or view: INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE. It is used to enforce complex business rules, maintain audit trails and keep data consistent. CREATE [OR REPLACE] TRIGGER trigger_name {BEFORE | AFTER | INSTEAD OF} {INSERT | UPDATE [OF column] | DELETE} ON table_name [FOR EACH ROW] [WHEN (condition)] BEGIN -- trigger body END; / Timing: BEFORE (fires before the event) and AFTER (fires after the event). Level: Row-level (FOR EACH ROW, fires once per affected row, can use :OLD and :NEW) and statement-level (fires once per statement). Event: INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE trigger"}];